# sanitize_exceptions.txt — findings you have decided to keep.
#
#   cp system/config/sanitize_exceptions.txt.template system/config/sanitize_exceptions.txt
#
# One extended-regex per line, matched against the guard's OUTPUT lines, which
# have the form  path/to/file.md:123:the matching text
# Blank lines and # comments ignored. The copy you create is gitignored.
#
# Why this file exists at all: a guard that fires on something correct teaches
# you to pass --no-verify, and a practitioner who has learned --no-verify has no
# guard. The honest fix is to record the decision, not to weaken the rule.
#
# Why it is not simply a list inside scripts/sanitize.sh: the exceptions name
# the very strings being protected. Writing them into tracked source would
# publish them — which is the defect the name check itself was rewritten to
# remove. Keep decisions here, mechanism there.
#
# Every run prints how many exceptions are active. That count is the thing to
# watch: it should be small, and each line should be a decision you remember
# making. Review it whenever it grows.
#
# WRITE THE PATTERN AGAINST THE TEXT, NOT THE WHOLE LINE. The path is part of
# every output line, so a bare token also matches any file whose *name* contains
# it — and then every finding in that file is exempt, including ones you never
# decided about. Require the match to fall after the line number:
#
#   ^[^:]*:[0-9]+:.*legacy-id          # the id, wherever it is mentioned
#   ^LICENSE:[0-9]+:Copyright          # or anchor to one path deliberately
#
# Prefer fixing the finding. Reach for this only when the finding is correct
# and the cost of changing it is real — a published identifier, a filename with
# inbound links, an artifact already in someone else's hands.
